Oniguruma in HomeRow
====================

Oniguruma (https://github.com/kkos/oniguruma) is the regular expression
library TextMate grammars are written for; VS Code uses it for the same
reason.  ICU's regular expressions (NSRegularExpression) are close but not
the same -- \h means something else, \G and back-references behave
differently at the edges -- and gnustep-base only has NSRegularExpression
when it was built with ICU.  One engine, identical on both platforms, is
worth two megabytes of source.

Taken from commit f95747b462de672b6f8dbdeb478245ddf061ca53, unmodified:
src/ holds the files HomeRow compiles plus the data files unicode.c
#includes.  Left out: the other encodings (HomeRow hands it UTF-16LE, which
is what an NSString is made of), the POSIX and GNU API shims, onig_init.c.
config.h is HomeRow's own.  BSD 2-clause licence, see COPYING.

Compiled files (the rest of src/*.c is #included by unicode.c, and must NOT
be compiled on its own):

  regerror.c regparse.c regext.c regcomp.c regexec.c regenc.c regsyntax.c
  regtrav.c regversion.c st.c unicode.c ascii.c utf8.c utf16_le.c
  unicode_unfold_key.c unicode_fold1_key.c unicode_fold2_key.c
  unicode_fold3_key.c
